        { term: 'Law of Supply', definition: 'As price increases, quantity supplied increases (all else equal). Higher prices incentivize more production.', example: 'When oil hit $130/barrel in 2022, U.S. producers drilled 50% more wells. When it fell to $70, drilling slowed.' },
        { term: 'Equilibrium', definition: 'The price where quantity supplied equals quantity demanded, the natural market-clearing price with no shortage or surplus.', example: 'If coffee equilibrium is $4/cup: at $6, shops have unsold inventory (surplus). At $2, lines out the door (shortage).' },
        { term: 'Price Elasticity', definition: 'How sensitive demand is to price changes. Elastic: demand changes a lot (luxury goods). Inelastic: demand barely changes (necessities).', example: 'Insulin demand is extremely inelastic, diabetics need it regardless of price. Netflix demand is elastic, cancel if price rises.' }
