          options: [
            { label: 'Do nothing, the market will diversify supply chains if the risk is real', consequence: 'COVID proved that markets don\'t adequately price geopolitical supply chain risk. The 2021 chip shortage cost the auto industry alone $210 billion. If Taiwan\'s fabs were disrupted, the global economic cost would be $500 billion to $1 trillion annually. National security risks require government action, this is a market failure that private companies can\'t solve alone.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'Invest $50-100 billion over 10 years to build domestic semiconductor capacity, while also diversifying to allied nations (Japan, South Korea, Germany), and investing in next-gen chip R&D', consequence: 'This is the balanced approach: domestic capacity for security, allied diversification for resilience, and R&D for long-term competitiveness. The U.S. CHIPS Act ($52B) follows this model. It\'s expensive, but the alternative, total dependence on a single geopolitically vulnerable source for the technology that powers everything, is far more expensive if disrupted.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Stockpile 5 years of chip inventory and hope tensions de-escalate', consequence: 'Stockpiling is a temporary band-aid, not a strategy. Chips become obsolete in 2-3 years, so a 5-year stockpile is technically impossible for leading-edge chips. And it doesn\'t address the underlying vulnerability. Japan tried stockpiling rare earths after China restricted exports in 2010, it helped short-term but only investment in alternatives provided lasting security.', score: 1 }
          ]
