            { label: 'Implement a carbon tax that rises predictably over 15 years, use revenue to fund clean energy investment and support displaced fossil fuel workers, and create incentives for private sector innovation', consequence: 'This is the economist\'s approach. A predictable, rising carbon price lets markets efficiently allocate investment toward clean energy without government picking winners. Revenue funds the transition (worker retraining, community investment) and innovation (R&D tax credits). Canada, the EU, and others are implementing versions of this. It\'s the approach recommended by 3,500+ economists including 28 Nobel laureates.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Ignore climate change, the economic costs of transition outweigh the benefits', consequence: 'Swiss Re estimates unmitigated climate change would reduce global GDP by 23% by 2100, a loss of $23 trillion per year. The economic cost of inaction far exceeds the cost of transition. This isn\'t an environmental opinion, it\'s an economic calculation confirmed by virtually every mainstream economic analysis.', score: 1 }
