      1,
      'Correct! Japan\'s debt is sustainable because of unique conditions: (1) 90% held domestically (Japanese savers buy government bonds, no foreign creditors to flee), (2) BoJ keeps rates near 0% (interest costs are manageable), (3) persistent current account surplus (Japan earns from trade and foreign investments). This shows that debt-to-GDP alone doesn\'t determine sustainability, who holds the debt and the interest rate matter enormously.',
      'Japan demonstrates that debt sustainability depends on multiple factors, not just the ratio. Domestic ownership prevents capital flight, low interest rates keep servicing costs manageable, and trade surpluses provide external income. But Japan\'s model isn\'t replicable, most countries can\'t borrow from their own citizens at near-zero rates indefinitely.'
