        'Development economics: will this be another resource curse (Nigeria) or a success story (Norway/Botswana)? The answer depends entirely on institutional quality. Behavioral economics: citizens may develop "oil entitlement mentality," reducing work effort and innovation. Political economy: elites may capture oil revenues, increasing inequality and corruption. Exchange rates: the "Dutch disease", oil revenue drives up the currency, making agriculture and manufacturing uncompetitive, potentially destroying more jobs than oil creates.',
        'The policy response requires synthesizing all frameworks: (1) Create a sovereign wealth fund (save during booms, spend during busts, countercyclical fiscal policy). (2) Invest oil revenues in education, healthcare, and infrastructure (human capital development). (3) Diversify the economy to avoid Dutch disease (trade and industrial policy). (4) Establish transparent governance of oil revenues (institutional design). (5) Auto-enroll citizens in savings from oil dividends (behavioral economics nudge). The correct answer isn\'t found in any single chapter, it\'s found in combining them all.'
