            { label: 'Moderate rate increases (2-3 percentage points) combined with targeted supply-side measures: subsidize food production inputs (seeds, fertilizer), release strategic grain reserves, negotiate emergency grain import deals, and communicate clearly that the shock is temporary to anchor inflation expectations', consequence: 'This synthesizes monetary policy (moderate tightening prevents expectations from unanchoring), supply-side economics (addressing the root cause, food supply disruption), trade policy (emergency imports), and behavioral economics (clear communication to prevent panic buying and self-fulfilling inflation). You\'ve correctly identified this as primarily a supply shock requiring supply-side solutions.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Do nothing, inflation will resolve itself when global commodity prices normalize', consequence: 'At 12%, inflation is causing real suffering, especially for the poor who spend 50-70% of income on food. Inaction risks inflation expectations becoming unanchored (behavioral economics), a wage-price spiral, and political instability. Even temporary supply shocks require policy response to mitigate damage and prevent second-round effects.', score: 1 }
