            { label: 'Accept with conditions: phase in tariff reductions over 5-7 years to give domestic industries time to adapt, invest heavily in worker retraining and education, negotiate protections for infant industries with genuine growth potential, and create a trade adjustment fund for displaced workers', consequence: 'This synthesizes trade theory (comparative advantage benefits both sides), development economics (infant industry protection can work with sunset clauses), behavioral economics (gradual change reduces loss aversion and status quo bias), and political economy (transition support reduces political opposition). You\'ve designed a realistic trade policy that captures gains while managing the transition.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Accept immediately with zero protections, free trade benefits everyone equally', consequence: 'While trade creates net benefits, the adjustment costs are real and concentrated. Workers in uncompetitive industries lose jobs while the benefits are diffuse (slightly lower prices for everyone). Without transition support, the political backlash can reverse trade liberalization entirely, as seen with anti-globalization movements worldwide. Ignoring distributional effects is a common economist mistake.', score: 1 }
